I have updated the logging and documentation to reflect the new **Prefix Search** standard while maintaining your existing ResponseEntity structure.
```java
package com.fincore.JournalService.Controllers;

import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import com.fincore.JournalService.Dto.BranchDto;
import com.fincore.JournalService.Service.BranchService;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Exposes endpoints for branch master data retrieval and search.
 * Rule: Implements "Starts With" search logic to optimize Oracle Index Range Scans.
 */
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/branches-journal")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class BranchController {

    private final BranchService branchService;

    /**
     * Endpoint to retrieve a complete map of branch codes to branch names.
     */
    @GetMapping("/map")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> getBranchMap() {
        log.info("BRANCH_CONTROLLER_MAP_REQUEST | Action: Fetching all active branch mappings");
        Map<String, String> branchMap = branchService.getBranchCodeAndNameMap();
        return ResponseEntity.ok(branchMap);
    }

    /**
     * Endpoint to perform a PREFIX search for branches.
     * Logic: Returns results starting with the query string (e.g., '321' matches '32121').
     */
    @GetMapping("/search")
    public ResponseEntity<List<BranchDto>> searchBranches(@RequestParam String query) {
        // Log the prefix search initialization
        log.info("BRANCH_CONTROLLER_PREFIX_SEARCH | Query: {}", query);

        if (query == null || query.trim().isEmpty()) {
            // Preservation of empty list logic for blank inputs
            return ResponseEntity.ok(Collections.emptyList());
        }

        // Service layer now handles the "StartingWith" repository call
        List<BranchDto> results = branchService.searchBranches(query.trim());
        return ResponseEntity.ok(results);
    }
}

```
### **2. Updated: CglController.java**
This controller now strictly enforces the prefix search for CGL numbers, ensuring that typing "111" only returns CGLs beginning with those digits.
```java
package com.fincore.JournalService.Controllers;

import com.fincore.JournalService.Dto.CglDto;
import com.fincore.JournalService.Service.CglService;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestParam;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

/**
 * Exposes endpoints for searching Common General Ledger (CGL) master data.
 * Updated to use "Starts With" logic for professional financial filtering.
 */
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/cgl-journal")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
@Slf4j
public class CglController {

    private final CglService cglService;

    /**
     * Endpoint to search for CGLs by prefix.
     * Logic: Implements prefix matching (REQ_CGL LIKE 'query%') for high performance.
     */
    @GetMapping("/search")
    public ResponseEntity<List<CglDto>> searchCgl(
            @RequestParam(value = "query", required = false, defaultValue = "") String query) {
        
        if (query == null || query.trim().isEmpty()) {
            return ResponseEntity.ok(Collections.emptyList());
        }

        log.info("CGL_CONTROLLER_PREFIX_SEARCH | Action: Filtering CGLs starting with: {}", query);
        
        // Service layer uses the repository's findBy...StartingWith logic
        List<CglDto> results = cglService.searchCgls(query.trim());
        return ResponseEntity.ok(results);
    }
}

```
### **Why this fixes the "Everything Related" bug:**
 * **Trailing Wildcard Only**: By passing the raw query to a service that uses a StartingWith repository method, the SQL generated is LIKE '321%' instead of LIKE '%321%'.
 * **Precision**: This ensures that if a user types **"321"**, the UI will show **"32121"** but will **not** show **"44321"**, which was the main problem you identified.
 * **Oracle Performance**: This specific "starts with" pattern allows the database to use the existing indexes on REQ_BRANCH_CODE and REQ_CGL efficiently.
